.\" wgx.1 — manual for the wgxplore console. mdoc, OpenBSD section order.
.\" Keep in lockstep with the --help banner and the verbs in main.go.
.Dd August 10, 2026
.Dt WGX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm wgx ,
.Nm wgx-tui
.Nd the WireGuard estate console: sweep, join, explain
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm tui | Cm estate | Cm check | Cm show
.Nm
.Cm attach Ar interface Ar container
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a keyboard-driven console for kernel WireGuard across a fleet.
It reaches every host in an inventory over plain
.Xr ssh 1 ,
reads the WireGuard state each one reports, and joins the results into a
single view: every interface, every peer, handshake-fresh or long dead.
.Pp
.Nm
does not generate keys and cannot declare a network.
That is a deliberate boundary, not a missing feature.
Hosts mint their own keypairs at install time, so a private key never
crosses a filesystem or network boundary and dies with the machine that owned
it; a console that also minted keys would be a second, weaker identity system
holding every private key in one place.
Because it only reads,
.Nm
adopts whatever is already running — a cluster built by configuration
management, a hand-rolled
.Pa wg0.conf ,
a host it has never seen.
There is no daemon, no server, no agent and no state of its own.
.Pp
Run with no arguments,
.Nm
opens the native GUI, falling back to the terminal console when no display is
available.
.Nm wgx-tui
is the fully static terminal-only build.
.Ss Membership without a registry
Nothing writes down who is supposed to be in a WireGuard network, so
.Nm
derives it.
Every legitimate peer entry in a mesh is some other host's own interface, so
the union of the hosts' own public keys is the membership roster, and set
difference against it answers the questions an inventory would have:
.Bl -tag -width "one-way link"
.It Em orphan
A peer entry whose key belongs to no host in the sweep.
Either a destroyed node whose entry nobody reaped, or an off-estate client
such as a laptop or phone.
The handshake age distinguishes them: an orphan handshaking right now is
somebody's device, an orphan that has never handshaken is debris.
.It Em one-way link
Host A carries host B as a peer, and B has no entry for A.
The tunnel looks configured from one end and does not exist from the other.
No single-host tool can see this, because
.Xr wg 8
only ever reports one end.
.El
.Pp
This is sound only over a sweep that reached the whole estate.
A host that does not answer contributes no key, so every legitimate peer
pointing at it looks orphaned;
.Cm check
counts unreachable hosts and says so above the findings rather than
presenting the artefact as fact.
.Ss Planes
An interface name is not just a name here.
The installer builds four WireGuard planes on every node and
.Xr kube-network 8
builds two more on cluster members, and each has a job:
.Bl -tag -width "wg-mgmt" -compact
.It Cm wg0
enrollment only; carries no services, by design
.It Cm wg1
management \(em ssh and the configuration-management agent
.It Cm wg2
the Kubernetes backend
.It Cm wg3
storage replication
.It Cm wg-mgmt
cluster management plane
.It Cm wg-k8s
cluster Kubernetes plane
.El
.Pp
.Nm
groups the estate by that role, which is the one thing
.Xr wg 8
cannot do: it reports interfaces and byte counters, and has no idea ssh is
bound to wg1, so it cannot tell you that a stale peer there is why a node
stopped answering.
Reporting per plane also turns a wall of peer rows into a line an operator
can read during an incident \(em "management degraded on n4" rather than
forty hex keys.
.Pp
An interface the substrate does not recognise is classified as unclassified
and still reported.
This console is pointed at estates it did not build, and a hand-rolled
.Pa wg0.conf
is a legitimate thing to look at.
Planes that do not exist on this estate are omitted entirely.
.Ss Views
.Bl -tag -width "estate"
.It Cm tui
Force the terminal console.
Estate tree on the left, dossier on the right.
.It Cm estate
Print the whole estate as a text tree, for scripts, cron and remote checks.
.It Cm check
Summarise each plane, then report orphaned peers and one-way links.
Silent on a healthy estate apart from one line per plane, which is what makes
it usable in a loop.
.It Cm show
One-shot peer dossiers for the local host.
.El
.Ss Verbs
.Bl -tag -width "attach"
.It Cm attach
Move a live WireGuard interface into a running container's network namespace.
The encrypted socket stays anchored in the namespace where the interface was
created, so the tunnel keeps working while the interface lives inside the
container.
With
.Ic --network none
the tunnel becomes the container's only network.
The literal
.Xr ip 8
command is printed before it runs.
Requires root.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.config/wgx/hosts" -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/wgx/hosts
Explicit estate inventory, one ssh target per line.
Takes precedence over the ssh configuration.
.It Pa /etc/wgx/hosts
System-wide inventory, same format.
.It Pa ~/.ssh/config
Fallback inventory.
Git forges and
.Ic gh-*
aliases are skipped.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A non-zero status accompanies a human-readable message on standard error, so
the verbs compose with
.Ic &&
and
.Ic || .
.Pp
.Cm check
exits zero whether or not it finds anything; its findings are advisory, and a
sweep that could not reach part of the estate is not the same as a failure.
Match on its output when you want an alarm.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Look at the whole fleet at once:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wgx estate
.Ed
.Pp
Nightly reaping check — orphans that have never handshaken are almost
always peer entries for nodes that no longer exist:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wgx check | grep never && notify "stale WireGuard peers to reap"
.Ed
.Pp
Find the half-built tunnel after adding a node to a mesh, which is the
failure that leaves traffic flowing one way:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wgx check | grep "no return peer"
.Ed
.Pp
Restrict an inventory to the cluster, leaving the ssh configuration alone:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf '%s\en' node1 node2 node3 > /etc/wgx/hosts
wgx check
.Ed
.Pp
Give a running container exactly one network, the tunnel and nothing
else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
podman run -d --network none --name job image
wgx attach wg-lab job
podman exec job ip addr add 10.44.0.7/24 dev wg-lab
podman exec job ip link set wg-lab up
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zxplore 1 ,
.Xr ip 8 ,
.Xr ip-netns 8 ,
.Xr wg 8 ,
.Xr wg-quick 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 2026 as the sister console to
.Xr zxplore 1 .
It carried a declaration model until August 2026 —
.Cm net create ,
.Cm net add ,
.Cm net render ,
.Cm net up
— which generated every member's keypair and stored the private keys in one
file on one host.
That was removed when the console was folded into its parent project, whose
hosts already generate their own keys.
Losing the ability to create a network is what gave it the ability to read
every network.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Anthony Aq anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Reading WireGuard state requires
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.Nm
escalates the read as gently as it can: directly when root, then
.Xr sudo 8
.Fl n ,
then
.Xr pkexec 1 .
Unprivileged,
.Xr wg 8
writes nothing to standard output and still exits zero, so a console that did
not escalate would report an empty estate rather than a permission problem.
.Pp
A peer that is legitimately outside the inventory — a phone, a laptop, a
host you chose not to sweep — is reported as an orphan, because from the
sweep's position the two are indistinguishable.
Handshake age is the tell.
.Pp
The estate view works wherever
.Xr wg 8
runs, but the container attach is Linux-only: it depends on network
namespaces.
Runtime testing to date is Linux.
